 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, you should consider wearing an N95 mask today if you need to be outdoors for an extended period or if you belong to a vulnerable group.** \n\nSingapore is experiencing significant transboundary smoke haze. While the situation eased slightly from reaching \"Very Unhealthy\" highs, the air quality across the island remains in the **Unhealthy** range.\n\n### Air Quality Situation\n* **24-hour PSI:** Ranges from **139 to 184** (Unhealthy), with the highest readings in Central Singapore. \n* **1-hour PM2.5:** Ranging from **83 to 158 µg/m³**, placing most of the island in the \"Elevated\" band and Western Singapore in the \"High\" band. \n\n### Ministry of Health (MOH) Mask Guidelines for Haze\nWhether you actually need to put on a mask depends on what you are doing outside and your personal health:\n\n1. **Short Commutes & Indoors:** If you are a healthy adult, you generally **do not** need to wear a mask for short outdoor exposure (like walking from a bus stop to a building) or while indoors.\n2. **Prolonged Outdoor Activity:** All individuals are advised to reduce prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion. If you cannot avoid working or staying outside for several hours, wearing an **N95 mask** is recommended. \n3. **Vulnerable Individuals:** Seniors, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions (such as asthma or COPD) should avoid outdoor activity altogether. If they must go outside, they should wear an N95 mask. *(Note: Wearing an N95 mask increases the effort needed to breathe; vulnerable individuals or pregnant women who feel dizzy, uncomfortable, or short of breath while wearing one should take it off and head indoors).*\n\n**Important Note on Mask Types:** Normal surgical masks or cloth masks **do not** protect against fine haze particles (PM2.5). You must use a properly fitted **N95** (or equivalent EN-149 / KN95 respirator) mask to effectively filter out smoke particulates.\n\n*(Note: Regarding general illness protocols, masks are no longer legally mandated outdoors in Singapore, and are only compulsory in high-risk healthcare settings like inpatient hospital wards and emergency departments).*\n\nBefore heading out, check the latest 1-hour PM2.5 readings for your specific neighborhood on the **NEA website (haze.gov.sg)** or the **myENV app**, as winds and expected showers may shift the haze throughout the day.",
